      "gpio_interrupts": {
        "basis": "Shutter button. The firmware's key scanner reads GPIO 0.0 (S1) and 0.1 (S2) through the bank's data register (0xfff41014) and reports a key pressed when its level is 0, so both are active low. The firmware registers the two pads' interrupts as GIC 167 and 168, level-sensitive and routed to CPU0, through the GICv2 distributor. Its arm, dispatch and disarm paths write only the distributor, and it arms 167 while S1 is released, so each line is asserted while its pad is low.",
        "routes": [
          {
            "port": 0,
            "pin": 0,
            "line": 167,
            "active_high": false
          },
          {
            "port": 0,
            "pin": 1,
            "line": 168,
            "active_high": false
          }
        ]
      },
      "countdown_dials": {
        "basis": "Command dials. The firmware's dial table holds the rear dial (keys translated to CMD_RIGHT/CMD_LEFT) and the front dial (CMD2_R/CMD2_L), both read from a counter source. The dial setup allocates countdown channels 2/3 to the rear dial and 4/5 to the front dial and programs the first of each pair through the kernel's timer setup in mode 1 with edge field 3 (control 0x100d) and count 0x80000000. The dial poller reads the count (0xfff3c100 + 0x10n) and posts the first key per positive count change, the second per negative change, at most five per poll. Edges per detent, the counting direction and the edge pacing are not measured: one firmware step is one edge here, the increasing direction counts up, and edges are 1 ms apart.",
        "dials": [
          {
            "control": "rear_command_dial",
            "channel": 2,
            "phase_ticks": 54000,
            "rest_phase": 0
          },
          {
            "control": "front_command_dial",
            "channel": 4,
            "phase_ticks": 54000,
            "rest_phase": 0
          }
        ]
      },
      "byte_i2c_inputs": {
        "2": {
          "basis": "The X-T3 5.11 touch controller sits on I2C bus 2, the byte controller at 0xfff3f000 (interrupt 0x43). The touch chip is the only peer on the bus.",
          "touch_chip": {
            "address": 85,
            "report_register": 0,
            "heartbeat_register": 7174,
            "mode_register": 7168,
            "restart_register": 7177,
            "restart_value": 193,
            "writable_registers": [
              7170,
              7171,
              7175,
              7177,
              7179,
              7195,
              7196,
              7197,
              7198,
              7199,
              7203,
              7204,
              7209,
              7214,
              7215,
              7218,
              7219,
              7224,
              7228,
              7229,
              7230,
              7231,
              7321,
              7360,
              7361,
              7362,
              7363,
              7364,
              7365,
              7376,
              7168,
              7176
            ],
            "contact_ids": [
              1,
              2
            ],
            "input_ready_irq": 153,
            "ticks_per_second": 54000000,
            "basis": "Touch chip of X-T3 5.11 at seven-bit address 0x55 with 16-bit register addresses, high byte first. The writable registers are the 30 start-up configuration registers 0x1c02..0x1cd0 plus the mode register 0x1c00 (2 enables reports) and 0x1c08. The eleven-byte two-contact report starts at register 0, the heartbeat counter is register 0x1c06, and the input-ready edge is interrupt line 153. Contact IDs 1 and 2 and the 54 MHz virtual timebase are declared, not measured."
          }
        }
      },
      "spi_inputs": {
        "1": {
          "basis": "Declared catalog input.",
          "level": "low",
          "power_registers": {
            "ambient_celsius": 25,
            "basis": "Declared catalog input. Register 21 is the USB port detection result the firmware reads after writing 0x0b00 to register 20 when a cable arrives; it starts its USB device only when the low bits read 1 or 3. The value 1 is inferred from a body that starts its USB device with a host attached, not read from the controller, and is served whether or not a cable is present.",
